        <USE>Boil</USE>
        <FORM>Leaf</FORM>
        <TIME>60</TIME>
        <DISPLAY_TIME>60.0 min</DISPLAY_TIME>
        <NOTES>A cultivar developed in 1968 from Brewer's Gold by open pollination in Idaho. An open pollination means that the male hop is unknown. The alpha acid content is relatively high—11.5 to 13.5%—but its co-humulone content and its beta acid range are also high.</NOTES>
      </HOP>
      <HOP>
        <NAME>Summit (US)</NAME>
        <ORIGIN>United States</ORIGIN>
        <ALPHA>17.7</ALPHA>
        <BETA></BETA>
        <AMOUNT>0.007087375</AMOUNT>
        <DISPLAY_AMOUNT>0.25 oz</DISPLAY_AMOUNT>
        <USE>Boil</USE>
        <FORM>Leaf</FORM>
        <TIME>60</TIME>
        <DISPLAY_TIME>60.0 min</DISPLAY_TIME>
        <NOTES>Useful for barleywines, stouts and IPAs. Alpha acid 17-19%. Trademarked.</NOTES>
      </HOP>
      <HOP>
        <NAME>Columbus (US)</NAME>
        <ORIGIN>United States</ORIGIN>
        <ALPHA>15.0</ALPHA>
        <BETA></BETA>
        <AMOUNT>0.01417475</AMOUNT>
        <DISPLAY_AMOUNT>0.5 oz</DISPLAY_AMOUNT>
        <USE>Boil</USE>
        <FORM>Leaf</FORM>
        <TIME>40</TIME>
        <DISPLAY_TIME>40.0 min</DISPLAY_TIME>
        <NOTES>A high yielding, high alpha acid American bittering hop. Also known by the trade name Tomahawk. One of the "Three Cs" along with Cascade and Centennial. Like the others it is citrusy and slightly woody. Columbus has a very high amount of total oils, and can impart a 'resiny' quality to a beer. Substitutes for bittering: Nugget, Chinook. Substitutes for aroma and flavor: Cascade, Centennial.</NOTES>
      </HOP>
      <HOP>
